# Sara's text about herself, read closely and then used as the model for the child's own.
lesson language-about-me v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=8 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "About me"
  goal "Read a short text about someone in [[language.name]], then write one about yourself."

  look {
    scene 34x16 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        passage sara title="[[aboutme.title]]" text="[[aboutme.sara-text]]" width=32 numbers=true
      }
    }
    say """
      Sara writes about herself: her name and age, where she lives and with whom, what she looks
      like, what she is like, what she loves, what she does not like and why, her favourite food,
      and what she does at the weekend. Each sentence says one thing, and the little joining words,
      and, but and because, tie them together.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.about-me-name k=4
    }
    show language.about-me-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.about-me-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.about-me-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.about-me-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.about-me-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.about-me-write k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.about-me-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.about-me-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.about-me-write k=3
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-about-me-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.about-me-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A text about yourself: who you are, where you live, what you are like, what you like and why."
  }

  grown-ups """
    This lesson closes the six years of the language: the child reads a whole short text and writes
    one of their own. The wrong idea to watch for is copying Sara's sentences without changing the
    facts to the child's own, and writing about someone else with the endings for I. The writing
    piece is read by you: tick what you see on the list, and ask the child to read it aloud. From
    this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is
    not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by
    the device. If the child is stuck, go through Sara's text one sentence at a time, changing one
    fact in each. Off the page, keep the text and add a sentence to it each week. It meets England's
    key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "write phrases from
    memory, and adapt these to create new sentences, to express ideas clearly" and "read carefully
    and show understanding of words, phrases and simple writing"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign
    languages: "Can write about themselves and familiar and simple matters by using simple words and
    phrases and basic expressions for which they are sufficiently familiar with the sounds while
    refering to example sentences"; and CEFR A2, "Can describe in simple terms aspects of his/her
    background, immediate environment and matters in areas of immediate need". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[aboutme.reading-and-swimming]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[num.twelve]] and [[aboutme.in-seville]] and the words to write are [[aboutme.she-lives]] and [[aboutme.kind-for-a-girl]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[aboutme.because-it-is-cold]] and [[aboutme.she-goes-to-the-beach]] and the words to write are [[aboutme.favourite]], [[aboutme.saturdays]] and [[aboutme.delights]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
